## Rules Without a Guard Hook Here

Claude Code and Cursor enforce these with hooks. This agent runs none of them, so this text is the only guard and you must apply it yourself before every write and every reply.

### Code quality

- No emoji characters in source code
- No inline imports in Python (all imports at file top)
- No unnecessary one-liner docstrings
- Format every file you edit (ruff/prettier/gofmt/shfmt as available)

### Comments

Never write:

- Narrative comments restating the code (`// Set the user`, `# increment counter`). Delete them.
- One-line docstrings on trivial functions (`"""Get the user."""`). If the name is clear, the docstring is noise.
- Context-of-origin comments (`# added for the payment flow`, `// handles issue #123`). That belongs in the commit message, not source.
- Change-history comments (`# was: old_value = 5`, `// renamed from oldFunc`). Use git.
- Section banners (`# ==== Helpers ====`). Use file structure instead.
- Doc-comments that paraphrase the signature (`/** Get user by id */` above `getUserById`). Add real content (constraints, error modes, thread safety, examples) or delete.

A comment that stays should say why, usually with words like `because`, `so that`, `to avoid`, `to prevent`, `workaround for`, `invariant:`, `safety:`, `must`, `cannot`, `thread-safe` or `lock ordering`. Standard directives are fine: `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `# pylint:`, `# shellcheck disable=`, `// eslint-disable-next-line`, `// @ts-expect-error`, `// prettier-ignore`, `//go:generate`, `//nolint`, `#pragma`, `#[derive(...)]`, `@param`, `@return`, `TODO:`, `FIXME:`, `NOTE:`, `HACK:`, `SAFETY:`, `INVARIANT:`, shebangs.
